# Where the Fortnite catalog comes from

Everything here was read on **2026-09-23**, the day of Chapter 7 Season 4
("Fortnite Override", v42.20 of September 17 plus hotfixes). `catalog.json` in this
folder is the list; this file says where every fact and picture in it came from,
what is and is not certain, and how to refresh it. Each item in `catalog.json` also
carries its own `sources` and, where something is doubtful, an `uncertain` sentence.

**The owner asked for** "all the Fortnite modes and known guns and everything, and a
search bar for it" (D126). So the catalog is two kinds (the `kinds` form,
`../README.md`):

- **Modes** lead: every Epic-made mode that has a gun pool and is live today, each
  with `loadout: { "pool": [...] }`, the guns in it today. 12 modes in 6 families.
- **Guns** are carried: every weapon Fortnite has had since September 2017, vaulted ones
  included, each with `usedBy` naming the modes it is in today (`[]` = vaulted). 521 guns, 360 of them vaulted.
- The guns kind has `"all": "All guns"`: the app lists every gun on its own too. A gun's
  Smart Actions set in All guns run in every mode; those set on a mode's page run only in
  that mode, and only once the Core has been taught to recognise the mode.

**Pictures: publisher artwork only (decision D116).** Every picture in `weapons/` and
`modes/` is Epic Games' own image, downloaded from an Epic host (`cdn2.unrealengine.com`,
`cms-assets.unrealengine.com`, or `fn-creator-community-cdn.qstv.on.epicgames.com`, which
serves the images of Epic's own posts on communities.epicgames.com), cropped to the weapon
(or, for a mode, to the mode's key art at a wide shape), scaled to at most 480 x 300 and
saved as PNG. None came from a fan wiki (not even the community wiki used below for
facts), Fandom, fortnite.gg, fortnite-api or an image search. Epic publishes pictures on
coloured backgrounds, not cut-outs, so each keeps its background. They are Epic's
copyright (and some its partners': Disney, Marvel, Lucasfilm, The Simpsons...), used in
the app to identify the weapon, the risk the owner accepted in D116. Delete a `.png` and
set that item's `image` to `null` and the app shows the class symbol instead.

A picture shows the weapon under the name the game gives it. Where a later chapter
redrew a weapon and kept its name (Assault Rifle, Pump Shotgun, Drum Gun, Tactical
Pistol...) the one entry may show the earlier model: the Core recognises what the owner
teaches it from his own screen, never the catalog picture.

